\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 1E.1 \'97 Memory authority\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Possible approaches:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Each client keeps its own history.\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Each model or node maintains separate memory.\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 HAL Core owns one unified memory layer.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \strokec2 My recommendation:
\f0\b0  HAL Core should be the sole authority for persistent memory.\
Clients provide context, nodes process it, and Core decides what is written to or retrieved from HAL Memory. Models and nodes should be treated as stateless workers by default.\
That preserves continuity when HAL changes models or moves work between nodes.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 1E.2 \'97 Memory tiers\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 I recommend four layers:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26 \cf0 Working context\
Current request, conversation, and active workflow\
\
Session memory\
Temporary context for the current interaction or task\
\
Personal memory\
Long-term preferences, people, projects, routines, and prior decisions\
\
Knowledge archive\
Documents, emails, media, research, transcripts, and indexed files\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 Working and session memory can live on HAL Core. Persistent memory and large files should live on the NAS, with Core maintaining indexes and retrieval metadata.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 1E.3 \'97 What gets remembered\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 HAL should not permanently save everything by default. That creates noise, weakens retrieval, and becomes difficult to audit.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 My recommendation:
\f0\b0  use selective memory:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Explicitly requested memories are always saved.\
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Stable preferences and important decisions may be suggested or automatically saved.\
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Routine conversation remains session-only.\
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Raw source material can be archived without being treated as a personal fact.\
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Sensitive information receives stricter retention and access rules.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 \strokec2 1E.4 \'97 Retrieval behavior\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 HAL should retrieve context based on the inferred intent, not dump a large history into every prompt.\
For example, a coding question might retrieve the relevant repository decisions, while a dinner recommendation might retrieve dietary preferences and recent choices.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 My recommendation:
\f0\b0  Core performs scoped retrieval, ranks relevance, and sends only the necessary context to the selected model.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

