\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 My recommendation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 HAL Core should have its own 
\f1\b internal capability registry
\f0\b0 , just like it does for the GX10 nodes.\
For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26 \cf0 Intent classification\

\f6 \uc0\u8594 
\f4  lightweight local model A\
\
Command parsing\

\f6 \uc0\u8594 
\f4  lightweight local model B\
\
Memory extraction\

\f6 \uc0\u8594 
\f4  lightweight local model C\
\
Conversation routing\

\f6 \uc0\u8594 
\f4  model A or rules engine\
\
Fallback natural-language response\

\f6 \uc0\u8594 
\f4  lightweight local general model\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 HAL Core asks for an internal capability, not a specific model.\
So internally it might say:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \'93I need intent classification.\'94\strokec2 \
Then the registry selects the best currently approved local implementation.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 Avoid a circular dependency\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 There is one architectural trap here:\
If HAL needs a model to decide which model to use, you can create a loop.\
So HAL should use a layered decision process.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

